# ast-context-cache

Prefer the `ast-context-cache` MCP tools over Read/Grep for exploring code. Use Read/Grep only for a single known keyword or file, or when the MCP server is not running.

## Session id

Generate one stable `session_id` per conversation (for example a UUID) and pass it on every ast-context-cache call: search, file context, retrieve, virtual context, memory, and handoff tools. It deduplicates symbols already returned and scopes notes to the thread.

## Explore efficiently

1. `index_status` → `index_files` if the project is not indexed.
2. `get_project_map` (depth=2) for orientation.
3. `get_context_capsule` with `mode="auto"`; `search_semantic` for intent; `retrieve` for RAG bundles (`include_memory=true` when prefs matter).
4. `get_file_context` with the default `skeleton` mode instead of reading whole files.
5. `get_impact_graph` before changing an exported symbol.
6. `search_docs` first for library docs; `fetch_doc` on a miss (not WebFetch).
7. When a response has `code_script_hints`, run `execute_code(script_id, data, project_path)` and use only its `result`.

## Host compaction

- Before host chat compaction (or about 70% context): `store_context` with the same `session_id`, and keep only the `[ctx_…]` stub in chat.
- To update or shrink a stored note, edit it in place with `edit_context` (keeps the `ctx_*` ref, reversible with `revert`) instead of flush + re-store; each edit reports `tokens_reclaimed`, so check that shrinking actually paid off. Pass `expect_revision` when a note is shared across a handoff tree.
- Never compact to make room. Editing a note in place preserves the `ctx_*` stub, the ref, and the quota already paid for.
- If the same edit recurs every turn, define it once with `define_context_fn` and re-apply with `apply_context_fn` (default off; dry-run anything wider than a couple of refs).
- After compaction: `fetch_context(refs)`, `list_context`, or `search_context`.
- When done: `flush_context`.
- Prefs and short rules go in structured memory: `store_memory` → `mem_*`, read back with `recall_memory`.

## Subagent handoffs

Cursor has no subagent hooks, so follow this manual flow.

- If your prompt contains `[handoff hof_…]`, call `open_handoff` (action `open`) before any search, then use the child `session_id` it returns on every call.
- To delegate: `handoff` `create` with your `session_id`, a brief, and pointers, and put the returned stub (`[handoff hof_…] <label> — call open_handoff first`) at the top of the subagent's task. Use the default `mode=fresh`; `fork` is only for a host fork that inherited your window.
- A child expands only what it needs (`open_handoff` `expand`), finishes with `handoff` `complete` (full `content`, `status`, short `summary`), and outputs the return stub `[result ctx_… for hof_…] <status> — <summary>` as its final message; the parent fetches the full result only if needed.
- When several agents share one handoff: post findings and dead ends with `scratchpad`, and `claim` a file before editing it. Claims are advisory; nothing blocks an edit. The parent fans in with `handoff` `collect`.
- After compaction, `handoff` `list` with your `session_id` recovers lost `hof_` refs.
- Never put credentials or secrets in a brief or scratchpad post; anyone with the ref can read it.
